    "narrative": "The core of our lineup today is anchored by **Corbin Carroll**, who boasts our highest efficiency score at 81.14. Facing the hittable Pecko (xERA 6.19) provides an ideal environment for him to exploit the matchup; his profile remains our most reliable engine for SB production, which is vital given our strategy of chasing the league's scarcest category. Similarly, **Trevor Story** (75.93) is a prioritized start due to a favorable park factor (+16%) and a high-leverage matchup against Bassitt. We are also leaning into the speed-efficiency of **Jose Caballero** (74.41). While his spot at the bottom of the order carries a penalty, his dynamic platoon advantage against the lefty Ray allows him to maximize his value in our pursuit of stolen bases.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Austin Riley** (43.98), who nearly cracked the starting nine. Ultimately, he was relegated to the bench because he is facing the formidable Wheeler, whose skill profile drags down any opposing hitter\u2019s efficiency below our current threshold. The optimizer prioritized the more favorable matchups for **Gabriel Moreno** and **Vinnie Pasquantino**, both of whom benefit from poor pitching matchups against Pecko and Scherzer respectively, pushing their scores well into the \"Strong\" territory.\n\nRisks for today center primarily on the \"Lineup Pending\" status of **Cole Young**, **Max Muncy**, and **Josh Naylor**. While we have projected them based on historical placement, any late-breaking shifts in the order could impact their efficiency. Furthermore, **James Wood** faces the toughest test of the day against Glasnow (xERA 2.91). Despite a high raw projection, the elite SP skill of the opposition acts as a drag on his output, though his elite xwOBA remains a reason for optimism. We are monitoring **Dalton Rushing** closely; despite a solid base projection, his lower position in the order and the matchup against Cavalli keep his score strictly in the marginal range.",
